DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  dduuppllooccaallee()  function creates a duplicate of the locale object re‐
       ferred to by _l_o_c_o_b_j.

       If _l_o_c_o_b_j is LLCC__GGLLOOBBAALL__LLOOCCAALLEE, dduuppllooccaallee() creates a locale object con‐
       taining a copy of the global locale determined by sseettllooccaallee(3).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, dduuppllooccaallee() returns a handle for the new locale object.  On
       error, it returns _(_l_o_c_a_l_e___t_) _0, and sets _e_r_r_n_o to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
       EENNOOMMEEMM Insufficient memory to create the duplicate locale object.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       POSIX.1‐2008.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       glibc 2.3.

NNOOTTEESS
       Duplicating a locale can serve the following purposes:

       •  To create a copy of a locale object in which one of more  categories
          are to be modified (using nneewwllooccaallee(3)).

       •  To  obtain  a  handle for the current locale which can used in other
          functions that employ a locale handle, such as  ttoouuppppeerr__ll(3).   This
          is done by applying dduuppllooccaallee() to the value returned by the follow‐
          ing call:

              loc = uselocale((locale_t) 0);

          This technique is necessary, because the above uusseellooccaallee(3) call may
          return the value LLCC__GGLLOOBBAALL__LLOOCCAALLEE, which results in undefined behav‐
          ior  if  passed  to  functions such as ttoouuppppeerr__ll(3).  Calling dduupplloo‐‐
          ccaallee() can be used to ensure that the LLCC__GGLLOOBBAALL__LLOOCCAALLEE value is con‐
          verted into a usable locale object.  See EXAMPLES, below.

       Each locale object created by dduuppllooccaallee() should be  deallocated  using
       ffrreeeellooccaallee(3).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       The  program below uses uusseellooccaallee(3) and dduuppllooccaallee() to obtain a handle
       for the current locale which is then passed to ttoouuppppeerr__ll(3).  The  pro‐
       gram  takes  one  command‐line argument, a string of characters that is
       converted to uppercase and displayed on standard output.  An example of
       its use is the following:

           $ ..//aa..oouutt aabbcc;
           ABC

   PPrrooggrraamm ssoouurrccee

       #define _XOPEN_SOURCE 700
       #include <ctype.h>
       #include <err.h>
       #include <locale.h>
       #include <stdio.h>
       #include <stdlib.h>

       int
       main(int argc, char *argv[])
       {
           locale_t loc, nloc;

           if (argc != 2) {
               fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s string\n", argv[0]);
               exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
           }

           /* This sequence is necessary, because uselocale() might return
              the value LC_GLOBAL_LOCALE, which can't be passed as an
              argument to toupper_l().  */

           loc = uselocale((locale_t) 0);
           if (loc == (locale_t) 0)
               err(EXIT_FAILURE, "uselocale");

           nloc = duplocale(loc);
           if (nloc == (locale_t) 0)
               err(EXIT_FAILURE, "duplocale");

           for (char *p = argv[1]; *p; p++)
               putchar(toupper_l(*p, nloc));

           printf("\n");

           freelocale(nloc);

           exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);
       }

